<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/calculator-papers.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-papers-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator-papers.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Claim glossary</span></nav><p class="kicker">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Claim Glossary for New Hampshire</h1><p class="lede">47 terms from policies, estimates and appraisal awards, in plain English. Type to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"><p class="hint" data-gloss-count aria-live="polite">47 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-W">W</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-A">Actual Cash Value (ACV)</dt><dd>Replacement cost minus depreciation. Many policies pay ACV first and the balance once repairs are finished.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Additional Living Expense (ALE)</dt><dd>The extra cost of living elsewhere while your home can’t be lived in after a covered loss, usually paid under Coverage D.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Amount of Loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the damage from a covered loss. It is the question an appraisal panel is set up to answer.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal Award</dt><dd>The written figure that settles the amount of loss, signed by any two of the three panel members.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal Clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either side demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Appraisal Demand</dt><dd>The written notice that starts appraisal, identifying the claim and stating that the parties disagree on the amount of loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-C">Competent and Disinterested</dt><dd>Common policy wording for an appraiser: someone qualified to value the loss and without a personal stake in the outcome.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage A (Dwelling)</dt><dd>Coverage for the house itself and structures attached to it, such as an attached garage or deck.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage B (Other Structures)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached buildings and structures, such as a barn, shed, fence or detached garage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage C (Personal Property)</dt><dd>Coverage for belongings and contents, often subject to special limits for items like jewelry or firearms.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Coverage D (Loss of Use)</dt><dd>Coverage for additional living expense or lost rent while the property is unusable after a covered loss.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-D">Declarations Page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy showing the insured property, coverages, limits, deductibles and policy period.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The portion of a covered loss you absorb before the insurer pays, either a flat amount or a percentage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and condition, subtracted from replacement cost to reach actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Detach and Reset</dt><dd>Removing an undamaged item to repair something else, then reinstalling it — gutters or a satellite dish, for example.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Drip Edge</dt><dd>Metal flashing along roof eaves and rakes that directs water away from the fascia and into the gutter.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-E">Examination Under Oath (EUO)</dt><dd>A recorded, sworn interview an insurer may request under many policies before it settles a claim.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-F">FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The New Hampshire FAIR Plan, the state’s residual-market property insurer for owners who can’t get standard coverage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation the insurer withholds until repairs or replacement are completed.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-I">Ice and Water Shield</dt><dd>A self-adhering waterproof membrane laid under shingles at eaves and valleys to resist ice-dam backup.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Ice Dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the roof edge that traps meltwater behind it, which can then back up under shingles and into the house.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-L">Line Item</dt><dd>One entry in an estimate, with a description, quantity, unit and price for a single task or material.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Whether repaired or replaced materials reasonably match undamaged areas in color, texture and appearance.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Microburst</dt><dd>A sudden, concentrated downdraft from a thunderstorm that can flatten trees and damage roofs along a narrow path.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, such as tarping, shutting off water or drying out a basement.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-N">New Hampshire Insurance Department</dt><dd>The state regulator in Concord, led by the Insurance Commissioner, which accepts consumer complaints and inquiries about insurers.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Nor’easter</dt><dd>A coastal storm with strong northeast winds that can bring heavy snow, rain, coastal flooding and widespread tree damage.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or Law Coverage</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs of meeting current building codes when damaged property is repaired or rebuilt, often with its own limit.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Overhead and Profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for coordinating a job, commonly applied when several trades are involved.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-P">Pitch</dt><dd>The steepness of a roof, written as inches of rise per 12 inches of run, such as 8/12.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Proof of Loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount you are claiming, which many policies require within a set time after the insurer asks.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-R">Recoverable Depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer pays once repairs or replacement are completed within the time the policy allows.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Replacement Cost Value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, before any depreciation.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>RSA Chapter 417</dt><dd>The New Hampshire law on unfair insurance trade practices, which includes a list of unfair claim settlement practices.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-S">Scope of Loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and the repairs each needs. Many appraisal disputes are really scope disputes.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Snow Load</dt><dd>The weight of snow and ice on a roof, which can strain or collapse framing when it builds up over a long winter.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Square (Roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of 100 square feet of roof surface, used to measure and price roofing.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Steep Charge</dt><dd>Extra labor cost for working on roofs above a certain pitch, often starting around 7/12 or 8/12.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Sublimit</dt><dd>A lower limit inside a coverage for a particular kind of loss or property, such as mold or debris removal.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Suit Limitation</dt><dd>A policy clause setting a window to sue the insurer. Enforceability can depend on New Hampshire law, so check the policy and ask an attorney.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an existing claim estimate, often after hidden damage is found during repairs.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel, who decides only the items the two appraisers can’t settle.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Underlayment</dt><dd>The protective layer between roof decking and shingles that serves as a second barrier against water.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-V">Vacancy Clause</dt><dd>Policy wording that limits or removes coverage for some perils after a building has been vacant or unoccupied for a set period.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt>Valley (Roofing)</dt><dd>The internal angle where two roof slopes meet, a spot where water concentrates and ice tends to build.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-W">Waste Factor</dt><dd>An allowance added to measured roofing or flooring quantities to cover cutting, starter rows and trim pieces.</dd></div><div class="g-item"><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors to price property claims line by line.</dd></div></dl></article>
